 "eras": [
  {"id":"17c","year":"17th c.","label":"Oldest standing ksar","short":"17th c.","lens":"none","summary":"The walled earthen ksar on the Ounila at the date UNESCO gives for its oldest standing buildings, with the fortified granary (agadir) crowning the hill, shown as the present mapped layout carried back.","basis":"UNESCO (list 444): the oldest constructions are \"not earlier than the 17th century\"; Wikipedia: fortified since the 11th century (Almoravid) but no standing building thought older than the 17th; CRATerre: the granary is the first construction, earliest written records 18th c.","conjecture":"No plan or dated image was found. The mapped granary, wall fragments, gate footprint and house footprints are carried back as inferred; the ksar has since been repaired and heavily restored, so footprints are not proof of the original plan. Which footprints are kasbahs, the caravanserai, the mosque and the Jewish cemetery are not mapped.","sources":[],"facts":["UNESCO: houses crowd within defensive walls reinforced by angle towers, entered through a baffle gate; some are \"small urban castles with tall corner towers\".","Wikipedia and CRATerre: a large fortified communal granary (agadir, ighrem n iqddarn) crowns the hill at about 1,300 m and is considered the first construction.","The sources date no individual building; \"17th c./12th c. AH\" is Archnet's event entry."]},
  {"id":"1987","year":"1987","label":"World Heritage inscription","short":"1987","lens":"none","summary":"The ksar as inscribed on the UNESCO World Heritage List (criteria iv and v) in 1987, mostly abandoned, with the Sidi Ali ou Amar sanctuary and the Muslim cemetery beyond the walls.","basis":"Archnet and Wikipedia give the inscription year 1987. CRATerre: residents moved to a new village across the river in the 1960s; over 90 families lived in the ksar in the 1940s (Archeyes). The mapped ksar is carried back; the new village is not drawn.","conjecture":"No plan of 1987 was found; the present mapped footprints are carried back. How much of the fabric stood or had been restored in 1987 is not documented, so the schematic layer draws the same houses as the other eras. The granary is shown as remains.","sources":[],"facts":["UNESCO criteria: (iv) an eminent example of a southern Moroccan ksar showing the main types of earthen construction; (v) a traditional earthen habitat made vulnerable by socio-economic change.","The filming history (from 1962) and the restoration it helped to pay for are in Wikipedia; no date for any single restoration was read."]},
  {"id":"today","year":"Today","label":"Heritage ksar","short":"Present","lens":"none","summary":"The ksar with \"several families\" still living in it, the new village across the river, the 2011 pedestrian bridge, hotels and cafes, and the ruined agadir on the hilltop.","basis":"The OSM-mapped granary, wall fragments, gate footprint, about 70 ksar footprints, the Kasbah Aït Ougram, the sanctuary, cemetery and the new-village buildings in the 900 m window.","conjecture":"Every height is unknown (kerb-high on the documented layer); see the constructed layer. The 2011 footbridge is not mapped in this window and is not drawn. The 2023 earthquake damage (Wikipedia: cracks and partial collapses) is not mapped.","sources":[],"facts":["Wikipedia: the ksar is only sparsely inhabited by \"several families\"; most residents live in the village across the river; a footbridge opened in 2011.","Wikipedia: the September 2023 earthquake caused cracks and partial collapses, with risk of further collapses.","CRATerre (2008): restoration covered the towers of five kasbahs and their decorations, the mosque, lanes and covered passages."]}
 ],
